<text x="185.42" y="35.56" size="1.27" layer="88">I2C Address 0x20
Setting Ax pins allows
selecting different addresses
if needed</text>
<text x="96.52" y="81.28" size="1.27" layer="88">500mA output capability,
more than enough to run ESP32 and peripherals</text>
<text x="10.16" y="78.74" size="1.27" layer="88">Only used if/when USB programming
isn't available for some reason</text>
<text x="99.06" y="38.1" size="1.27" layer="88">Keep pressed when plugging USB
to enter firmware download mode</text>
<text x="38.1" y="17.78" size="1.27" layer="88">Data interface and power supply
(5V 500mA)</text>
<text x="157.48" y="22.86" size="1.27" layer="88">Not all I/Os fit on the ESP32 itself,
so an external expander is used.
2nd port (not used here) has 
8 more I/Os available</text>
<text x="165.1" y="228.6" size="1.27" layer="88">Pins marked with warning signs
are either special function or
strapping pins.
Refer to ESP32-S3 docs for restrictions
on these special/strapping pins.</text>
<text x="276.86" y="248.92" size="1.27" layer="88">All inputs are pulled up, so are active low. Buttons should make contact to GND when pressed.
TVS diodes are included for ESD protection but not strictly necessary.</text>
<text x="2.54" y="256.54" size="5.08" layer="94">ESP32 SimButtonBox</text>
<text x="2.54" y="246.38" size="2.54" layer="94">Self-powered USB device, implementing
a standard USB game controller profile.</text>
